{"id": "8ad10622-6566-4fda-ac03-7bd1d0504a9d", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Long-term Bedrock API keys are really IAM service-specific credentials scoped to bedrock.amazonaws.com, so finding them means listing service-specific credentials per IAM user across accounts — that's token-heavy work I should hand off to a subagent rather than doing myself, especially since this is a straightforward read-only inventory task rather than a root-cause investigation.

I want the subagent to gather status, creation date, expiration, age, and usage data (likely via CloudTrail lookups since there's no direct last-used API for these credentials), plus context like attached IAM policies and whether the user matches the Bedrock-console-created naming pattern. Since the user wants recommendations on which keys to remove rather than actual deletion, I'll outline my plan briefly and then spawn a subagent with these instructions.

"}]}

{"id": "618b2c94-14ee-48f3-ab71-e9713f1bf084", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll scan every associated AWS account for long-term Bedrock API keys, which are IAM service-specific credentials for Bedrock. I'll check each key's status, age, expiry, recent use and the permissions on its IAM user. Then I'll recommend which to remove.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_cfOjUY4mnDc36nWCYmCKUk", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-api-key-audit", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["investigating-infrastructure-changes", "verifying-with-docs", "discovering-topology"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Produce a complete inventory/audit of every long-term Amazon Bedrock API key across ALL AWS accounts associated with this agent space (use list_associations / get_associations to enumerate every account, including monitor and source accounts; agent space home account is 012345678901_1, region us-east-1). READ-ONLY: do not attempt any mutating call.",
  "context": "Long-term Bedrock API keys are IAM service-specific credentials with ServiceName bedrock.amazonaws.com, belonging to IAM users (the console typically creates users named like BedrockAPIKey-xxxx, but keys can be attached to any IAM user). IAM is global; query it per account (us-east-1 endpoint is fine). The user wants a full report and a recommendation on which keys should be removed. Be exhaustive: iterate over ALL IAM users in each account (paginate), call iam list-service-specific-credentials with service-name bedrock.amazonaws.com for each user (or without filter and filter), and record every credential. Report any account you could not access and why, so the report is not silently incomplete.",
  "criteria": "For each key capture: account ID, IAM user name, ServiceSpecificCredentialId, ServiceUserName, Status (Active/Inactive/Expired), CreateDate, ExpirationDate (or 'never expires'), age in days vs now (2026-11-24). Per owning IAM user capture: attached managed policies and inline policies (flag overly broad ones like AdministratorAccess, bedrock:*, *:*, or anything beyond AmazonBedrockLimitedAccess), permissions boundary, tags, whether the user also has console password/access keys/MFA, and whether the user looks purpose-created for Bedrock. Determine last use / recent activity where possible via CloudTrail (read the investigating-infrastructure-changes skill and use lookup_cloudtrail_events for events by that IAM user, e.g. bedrock InvokeModel/Converse calls, and CreateServiceSpecificCredential events to find who created each key and when; note CloudTrail lookup only covers ~90 days of management events, and data events like InvokeModel may not be present — state that limitation). Also check for CloudTrail/IAM evidence of keys being created recently by unexpected principals. Then classify each key as REMOVE / REVIEW / KEEP with a short evidence-based reason. Suggested removal criteria: Inactive or Expired status; no expiration set (never expires) and older than ~90 days; no observed usage in 90 days; excessive permissions; owner user unused/orphaned; created by unexpected principal. Also note account-level findings (e.g. SCP or policy guardrails against creating long-term keys, count of keys per account) and recommend short-term keys/IAM roles as the preferred alternative (verify any documentation claims you make about Bedrock API keys via verify_aws_claim). Return a concise structured result: summary counts, a table of all keys with classification, per-account notes, access gaps/limitations, and the recommended removal list."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit Bedrock long-term API keys across associated AWS accounts"}], "created_at": null}

